---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout description="Lowcountry Digital Works builds practical websites, applications, automation, and technical systems with maintainability and client ownership in mind.">
  <section class="section hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Practical. Maintainable. Yours.</p>
      <h1>Useful technology, built to remain yours.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Lowcountry Digital Works builds and improves websites, applications, automation, and digital systems with an emphasis on maintainability, named access, clear documentation, and straightforward handoff.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/services/">Explore services</a><a class="button button--secondary" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">What I build</p><h2>Practical systems for real business needs.</h2>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        <article class="card"><p class="card__kicker">Web</p><h3>Websites</h3><p>Fast, accessible business sites designed to be easy to maintain and portable.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><p class="card__kicker">Applications</p><h3>Custom applications</h3><p>Focused tools for workflows that off-the-shelf software does not solve cleanly.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><p class="card__kicker">Automation</p><h3>Workflow automation</h3><p>Reduce repetitive work and connect practical processes without unnecessary platform sprawl.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="actions"><a href="/services/">See all six service areas</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--navy">
    <div class="container principle">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Ownership principle</p><h2>Your business should own its digital assets.</h2><p class="lede">Company domains, repositories, hosting, and other critical systems should be held in the right owner accounts, with named role-based access for the people who manage them.</p></div>
      <ul class="principle__list"><li>Client-owned accounts where practical</li><li>Named access instead of shared credentials</li><li>Portable repositories and open standards</li><li>Documented handoff, recovery, and rollback</li></ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2>Assess → Build → Handoff</h2><div class="flow"><article class="flow__step"><h3>Assess</h3><p>Understand the real need, current systems, constraints, ownership, and cost before choosing technology.</p></article><article class="flow__step"><h3>Build</h3><p>Use the simplest maintainable architecture that solves the problem, with accessibility and security treated as normal quality requirements.</p></article><article class="flow__step"><h3>Handoff</h3><p>Leave clear documentation, named access, recovery information, and a system that can be maintained without artificial lock-in.</p></article></div></div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section--warm"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Lowcountry roots</p><h2>Local perspective, not a geographic limit.</h2><p class="lede">Lowcountry Digital Works is being established in South Carolina's Lowcountry and can support practical digital work beyond the local area.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button--secondary" href="/about/">About Lowcountry Digital Works</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container readable"><p class="eyebrow">Contact</p><h2>Have a system that needs to work better?</h2><p class="lede">Email Eddie with the problem you are trying to solve. A simple description is enough to start.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="mailto:eddie@lowcountrydigitalworks.com">eddie@lowcountrydigitalworks.com</a></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
